WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:03.340
Narrator: Take a look at
this shot from "The Shining."

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:05.165
A pretty innocent scene.

00:00:05.165 --> 00:00:06.481
But when we zoom out,

00:00:06.481 --> 00:00:09.410
the space suddenly becomes menacing.

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:13.070
That's partly because compositions
like this stress us out.

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:15.140
All that empty, or negative, space

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:17.080
tells us there's something lurking

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.758
and that this character is in danger.

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:23.680
Negative space is just one of many tools

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:26.560
horror-film makers use
to play on your senses

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:28.480
without you even noticing.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.170
Let's take a look at
some of the hidden ways

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:33.370
horror movies manipulate
you into being scared.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:37.380
You might have heard
about nonlinear sounds.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.318
[shrill, suspenseful music]

00:00:39.318 --> 00:00:40.740
[screams]

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.150
We are conditioned to respond

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:44.920
to those screeching strings with fear.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:48.302
But the opposite of that
can be even more unsettling.

00:00:48.302 --> 00:00:50.890
[growling]
[screaming]

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:52.353
Hear that low rumble?

00:00:52.353 --> 00:00:54.350
[growling]
[screaming]

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:56.480
You might've felt it
more than you heard it.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:59.910
That's because this scene uses
super-low-frequency sounds

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:02.680
that are right at the limit
of what humans can hear.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:05.330
The human ear is best
at registering sounds

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:08.240
above the frequency of 20 hertz.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:10.830
Anything below that is called infrasound.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:12.840
Horror movies like "Paranormal Activity"

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.930
use sounds right around
that 20-hertz threshold,

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:18.550
called sub-bass sounds.

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:20.040
It's these deep rumblings

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.570
that ratchet up the
tension in key moments.

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:26.590
According to some studies,
we also often feel them,

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:30.840
experiencing the sound waves
in our bodies as vibration.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.750
People subjected to
infrasound and sub-bass sounds

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:36.991
reported feelings of
discomfort, even chills,

00:01:36.991 --> 00:01:40.274
or worse, like when director Gaspar Noé

00:01:40.274 --> 00:01:42.360
used nonstop sub-bass sounds

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:45.369
through the first 30 minutes
of his movie "Irreversible."

00:01:45.369 --> 00:01:48.930
[ambient tones rumbling]
[men speaking indistinctly]

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:51.040
The BBC reported that 20 people

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:53.860
at the Cannes Film Festival
fainted during the film

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:55.690
and needed medical attention.

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:57.800
These sounds operate at the outer edges

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:00.410
of your hearing range to
make you uncomfortable

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:02.310
without you even knowing why.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:05.333
And this idea can apply to
your visual senses, too.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:09.380
Did you catch that?

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:12.080
This white face is one
of several hidden shots

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:13.840
of the demon in "The Exorcist."

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:16.500
They are often referred
to as subliminal images,

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:18.070
which isn't really accurate,

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:20.970
since we are able to
consciously perceive them.

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:22.250
But each image only lasts

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:24.410
about an eighth of a second on screen,

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.150
less than a blink of an eye.

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:27.560
Research shows that people

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:30.240
presented with threatening
images, even briefly,

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:33.110
become faster at identifying
additional threats.

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:34.810
That state of hypervigilance

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:37.190
is right where horror movies want you.

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:39.280
So as each flash comes on screen,

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:41.010
you get better at catching them,

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:44.200
but you might also question
whether you saw anything at all.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:45.570
And that's the point.

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:46.540
At the end of "Psycho,"

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:49.120
director Alfred Hitchcock
placed this image

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:52.240
of Mrs. Bates' skull
over Norman Bates' face,

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:55.520
but didn't include this detail
in all prints of the movie.

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:57.878
So in theaters, you
might've seen the skull

00:02:57.878 --> 00:03:00.780
in one screening and not in the next.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:03.580
The American remake of "The
Ring" played a similar trick

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:06.160
by inserting shots from
the cursed videotape

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:08.950
as flash frames in different
scenes of the movie,

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:12.550
but varying the frames in
different versions of the film.

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:14.780
These fleeting images and, more generally,

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:16.610
the popular horror-movie style

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:18.600
of quick-cutting between shots

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:19.920
work to disorient you

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.928
and make you question your own perception.

00:03:24.455 --> 00:03:25.396
Casey: Hello?

00:03:25.396 --> 00:03:27.293
Killer: Why don't you want to talk to me?

00:03:27.293 --> 00:03:28.660
Narrator: See all this?

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:31.470
That's called negative
space: anything in the frame

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:34.200
that's not the subject we're focusing on.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.900
Movies traditionally go
for a harmonious balance

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:39.080
of positive and negative space.

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:42.120
The subject is placed at
the rule-of-thirds lines,

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:43.300
and there's more space

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.180
in the direction the subject's facing,

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:47.950
allowing what's called lead room.

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:50.530
Horror movies deliberately
violate those rules

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:52.650
to make audiences squirm.

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:53.980
Sometimes they'll put the subject

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:56.040
almost on the edge of that frame line,

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.820
so the blank space overwhelms the frame,

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:01.700
or they'll leave more empty
space behind the subject,

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:03.700
which makes us wonder
if something's going to

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:04.933
sneak up on them.

00:04:04.933 --> 00:04:06.410
Camera movements in horror movies

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:09.500
often emphasize the
negative space even more.

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:12.330
See the slow pans in these scenes?

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:15.770
Or in these scenes, how the
camera lingers on empty spots?

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:17.236
In horror movies, you'll sometimes see

00:04:17.236 --> 00:04:19.550
the camera actually leave the subject

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:21.460
and track through a vacant room,

00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:24.520
filling the shot with only negative space.

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:26.350
Leaving all this openness in the frame

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:29.460
lets the audience's
imagination fill in the blanks.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:33.230
We're left wondering if or
when some attacker will pop up

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:35.080
and from which direction.

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:38.410
Extreme wide establishing
shots can also draw attention

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:41.440
to how alone and vulnerable
the protagonists are

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:43.640
and how remote the setting is.

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:44.670
How many times have you seen

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:47.050
shots like this in horror movies?

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:48.650
They work so well to set the scene

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:51.210
because they play on some
of our most primal fears

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:53.690
of isolation and defenselessness.

00:04:53.690 --> 00:04:56.820
So if too much empty space freaks us out,

00:04:56.820 --> 00:05:00.296
what happens when the shot
has almost no space at all?

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:04.710
It can actually amp up the fear even more,

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:07.700
especially in climactic scenes.

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:09.140
In this scene from "The Nun,"

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:12.410
we see a shot of Valak
rise up from the water,

00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:15.170
then go to a close-up of the protagonist

00:05:15.170 --> 00:05:17.060
looking on in horror.

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:19.200
By the time we cut to a wide shot again,

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:21.183
Valak's now fully out of the water,

00:05:21.183 --> 00:05:23.091
towering over Irene.

00:05:23.091 --> 00:05:25.560
This is a common pattern in horror movies.

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:26.590
They'll show you the monster

00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:28.410
approaching or coming into view,

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:32.120
then cut to a tight close-up
of the protagonist reacting.

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:33.710
It's terrifying because we know

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:35.930
the monster's advancing or attacking,

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:38.230
but we're not allowed to see it happening.

00:05:38.230 --> 00:05:40.030
We're only witnessing it secondhand

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:42.130
through the protagonist's response.

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:44.400
More generally, tight
shots on the protagonist

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:46.680
make it so you can't see what's going on,

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:48.800
like in this scene from "Psycho."

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:51.600
Notice the camera's tight
on Detective Arbogast

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:52.820
as he goes up the stairs,

00:05:52.820 --> 00:05:56.290
so we can't see what danger
he might be walking into.

00:05:56.290 --> 00:06:00.165
That leaves us feeling as
helpless and on edge as he is.

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:05.010
Another way of keeping
audiences in the dark

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:07.530
is by manipulating camera focus.

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:09.080
In this scene from "Donnie Darko,"

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:11.700
we go from a shot of Jake
Gyllenhaal from behind

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:15.400
to an out-of-focus medium
close-up of his face.

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:18.690
As he slowly walks into
focus, he becomes crisper,

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:20.830
but the background remains blurry,

00:06:20.830 --> 00:06:24.230
leaving us wondering what
might be lurking there.

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:26.880
That shot uses shallow focus.

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:29.750
That's when the aperture of
a camera's lens is wide open,

00:06:29.750 --> 00:06:32.080
creating a shallow depth of field.

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:34.330
This allows filmmakers to finely control

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:37.370
which people or objects
in the frame stay sharp.

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:39.890
In other genres, filmmakers
use shallow focus

00:06:39.890 --> 00:06:43.170
to direct our attention to
the in-focus part of the shot.

00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:46.010
But horror-film makers
weaponize shallow focus

00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:48.230
to make us uncomfortable.

00:06:48.230 --> 00:06:50.810
Let's look at another creepy
Jake Gyllenhaal scene,

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:52.770
this one from the movie "Enemy."

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:54.210
Here are the two subjects:

00:06:54.210 --> 00:06:56.330
Mélanie Laurent, who's being followed,

00:06:56.330 --> 00:06:58.320
and Jake, who's following her.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:00.190
Both are equally important,

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:02.040
but one's in the sharp foreground,

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:04.210
and the other's in the blurry background.

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:07.050
This leaves us unsure
where to direct our eyes,

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:09.130
and it forces us as audience members

00:07:09.130 --> 00:07:12.505
into an uncomfortable
and hyperattentive state.

00:07:14.390 --> 00:07:17.230
Here's another key way horror
movies play with perspective.

00:07:17.230 --> 00:07:19.440
Jill: Hi, it's Jill, the babysitter.

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:20.690
Narrator: This is a framing device

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:22.760
sometimes called the dark voyeur

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:25.250
or hidden onlooker perspective.

00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:27.520
The camera might shoot
from behind something,

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:29.530
a bush or a piece of furniture,

00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:32.220
or through something, like a window.

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:33.500
This perspective puts us

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:36.170
in the uncomfortable
position of being voyeurs,

00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:38.950
where we know something that
the protagonist doesn't:

00:07:38.950 --> 00:07:40.600
that they are being watched.

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:42.590
The shot can also separate the audience

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:44.093
from the actual scene.

00:07:44.093 --> 00:07:46.720
Here, for example, we're
looking on from another room.

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:49.635
That framing makes us
hyperaware of our powerlessness

00:07:49.635 --> 00:07:51.570
to stop what's about to happen.

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:53.600
We can only watch, complicit,

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:56.925
which can make for a pretty
torturous viewing experience.

00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:00.230
If the dark voyeur perspective

00:08:00.230 --> 00:08:02.550
turns the audience into observers,

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:04.840
the first-person-killer perspective

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:07.130
turns us into accomplices.

00:08:07.130 --> 00:08:09.360
That's the classic slasher "Halloween,"

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:12.300
opening with a shot from the
murderer's point of view.

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:14.110
The first-person-killer perspective

00:08:14.110 --> 00:08:16.240
is a hallmark of slasher movies,

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:19.290
popularized by the earliest
films in that genre.

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:21.356
The first-person-victim perspective

00:08:21.356 --> 00:08:23.140
can be just as powerful,

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:25.660
because it literally puts
us in the character's shoes

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:27.870
as they approach danger.

00:08:27.870 --> 00:08:30.868
That's why many horror
movies use a shaky cam.

00:08:30.868 --> 00:08:31.818
Friend: Jason, what are you doing?

00:08:31.818 --> 00:08:34.300
Narrator: Or a zooming effect
like the kind in "Vertigo,"

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:37.310
which makes us feel as dizzy and uneasy

00:08:37.310 --> 00:08:39.740
as the protagonist does in the scene.

00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:41.860
Both types of first-person-POV shots

00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:43.680
are effective for a few reasons.

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:47.730
For one, they hide crucial
information about the scene.

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:49.840
In the second half of
"The Blair Witch Project,"

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:52.080
the more danger the protagonists are in,

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:55.100
the lower and more shakily
they hold the camera,

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:58.460
never focusing the lens
on anything for very long.

00:08:58.460 --> 00:09:00.200
In the opening shot of "Halloween,"

00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:03.350
Michael Myers' mask is
partially covering the camera,

00:09:03.350 --> 00:09:05.850
so we're not privy to
everything in the frame.

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:07.090
Even more importantly,

00:09:07.090 --> 00:09:10.490
we don't know the identity
of this unseen intruder.

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:12.170
That's also why Steven Spielberg

00:09:12.170 --> 00:09:14.780
used the first-person-POV shots in "Jaws":

00:09:14.780 --> 00:09:16.660
to hide the shark from the audience,

00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:19.977
which famously worked to make
the monster even scarier.

00:09:22.490 --> 00:09:26.630
All these techniques combine
into an assault on our senses.

00:09:26.630 --> 00:09:28.500
They're part of the visual language

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:29.920
horror movies have developed

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:32.250
to make us doubt our own senses,

00:09:32.250 --> 00:09:33.980
prepare for an attack,

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:36.400
and fully immerse ourselves in the scene

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:39.750
through the eyes of
both killer and victim.

